        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Can You Still Buy a Nautilus Treadmill? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Not new. Nautilus treadmills are sold only second-hand now. Do not buy a used T616 or T618
            without checking the serial number against the 2022 recall, which has no remedy left. For a new
            machine from the same family, the Bowflex T6 and T9 are the closest replacements.</strong>
          </p>
          <p>
            On 5 November 2026 nautilus.com sold a <a href="/best-vibration-plates/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>vibration plate</a>, amino-acid capsules and apparel, and no
            treadmills. The company that made the T614, T616 and T618, Nautilus Inc., sold the Nautilus brand in
            May 2023, changed its own name to BowFlex Inc. later that year, and filed for Chapter 11 in March 2024.
            Johnson Health Tech bought BowFlex Inc.'s assets — the BowFlex, Schwinn and JRNY brands among them —
            in April 2024. It now sells three Bowflex treadmills and four Schwinn treadmills. None of them carries
            the Nautilus name.
          </p>
          <p>
            That leaves two kinds of buyer. If you are looking at a used Nautilus, the most important thing on this
            page is the recall section: about 7,300 T616 and T618 treadmills sold in 2021 can start on their own,
            and because the company that issued the recall no longer exists, there is no repair. If you simply liked
            what Nautilus treadmills offered — a folding 20 by 60-inch deck, 15% incline, 12 mph and a long frame
            warranty for around $1,000 to $1,500 — the Bowflex and Schwinn cards below are the current versions of
            that idea. We have not tested any of these treadmills.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-happened" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What Happened to Nautilus, and Who Owns It Now?</h2>
<p>
            Nautilus began as the name of Arthur Jones's strength machines in the 1970s and became, through
            acquisitions, the parent company of Bowflex, Schwinn Fitness and, later, the JRNY app. For most of the
            2010s Nautilus Inc. of Vancouver, Washington, sold <a href="/home-cardio-equipment-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>home cardio equipment</a> under both the Nautilus and
            Schwinn names, and the T-series treadmills were its Nautilus-branded line. The commercial Nautilus
            strength business had been sold years earlier and is made under licence by Core Health &amp; Fitness,
            which is why gym-floor Nautilus machines are unrelated to the home treadmills.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The 2023 brand sale and the BowFlex rename</h3>
          <p>
            In May 2023 Nautilus Inc. announced that it had sold its namesake brand. Trade press reported the price
            as about $13 million, used to pay down a loan, and the company said it would concentrate on Bowflex,
            Schwinn and JRNY, keeping a licence to sell remaining Nautilus-branded stock. In late 2023 it became
            BowFlex Inc. Trademark filings in 2025 for the Vibratone vibration plate name a Nautilus Brands LLC of
            Wayne, New Jersey, and nautilus.com's footer now reads "Nautilus + Vibratone". We could not find a
            primary source naming the 2023 buyer, so we do not name one here.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Johnson Health Tech and Bowflex</h3>
          <p>
            BowFlex Inc. filed for Chapter 11 in March 2024 with Johnson Health Tech as the stalking-horse bidder at
            $37.5 million. The court approved the sale that April, and coverage of the deal lists the BowFlex,
            Schwinn and JRNY brands, inventory and parts. It is often said that the Nautilus treadmills "went to
            Johnson Health Tech with Bowflex"; on the evidence we found, that is not right. The Nautilus brand had
            already been sold a year earlier, and Johnson Health Tech sells no Nautilus-branded treadmill.
          </p>
          <p>
            In practice, the people and designs carried on. Johnson Health Tech also owns <a href="/horizon-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Horizon</a>, <a href="/matrix-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Matrix</a> and Vision, and coverage of the
            current Bowflex T9 notes that it uses Horizon-style drive technology. Our <a href="/bowflex/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Bowflex brand guide</a> covers what changed for
            Bowflex owners, including how warranties on older machines are handled.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="models" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Nautilus Treadmill Models: T614, T616 and T618</h2>
<p>
            Nautilus used a simple scheme: T for treadmill and a number that rose with the features. Three models
            account for nearly all Nautilus treadmills you will see for sale. Their figures come from old listings
            and reviews, which do not always agree; where they differ we say so, rather than pick one.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[560px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Model</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Deck</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Speed / incline</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Max user</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Recall status</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">T614</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">20 x 55 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">12 mph / about 12%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">300 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Not recalled</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">T616</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">20 x 60 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">12 mph / 12% or 15% (sources differ)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">300 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Units sold Apr-Nov 2021 recalled</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">T618</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">20 x 60 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">12 mph / 15%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">350 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Units sold Apr-Nov 2021 recalled</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Nautilus T618</h3>
          <p>
            The T618 was the top Nautilus treadmill and, with 480 searches a month, still the one people look for.
            A Nautilus specification page hosted on Schwinn's site (seen in search results) and TreadmillReviews.com
            agree on a 20 by 60-inch running surface, 0 to 12 mph, 0 to 15% motorized incline and a 350 lb maximum
            user weight. It folded with hydraulic assistance, had a backlit LCD console with 26 programs, Bluetooth,
            speakers and a fan. The motor is the main conflict: most reviews give 3.5 continuous horsepower, but one
            specification table gives 2.75 HP. So are the dimensions: the Schwinn-hosted page gives 78 by 37.6 by
            62.8 inches and 253 lb, TreadmillReviews.com 72.2 by 35.2 by 57.6 inches. TreadmillReviews.com lists the
            warranty as 15 years on frame and motor, five on parts and electronics and two on labour. The CPSC put
            its price at about $1,500; TreadmillReviews.com listed $1,299.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Nautilus T616</h3>
          <p>
            The T616 was the middle model and the most common. TreadmillReviews.com and USA Home Gym agree on a 3.0
            continuous horsepower motor, a 20 by 60-inch deck, 12 mph and a 300 lb user rating. They disagree on
            incline — TreadmillReviews.com says 15%, USA Home Gym's 2016 review 12% — which may reflect a mid-life
            update we cannot confirm. TreadmillReviews.com gives 10 years on frame and motor, three on mechanical and
            electrical parts and one on labour, a hydraulic fold and 26 programs. The CPSC gave its 2021 price as
            about $1,150.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Nautilus T614</h3>
          <p>
            The T614 was the entry model, with a shorter 20 by 55-inch deck and a 2.75 CHP motor, 12 mph and the same
            300 lb rating. It was discontinued before the others. Its incline is quoted as up to 12%, and its warranty
            is quoted inconsistently, so check any figure on a used unit against its manual. Being outside the recall,
            it is the only Nautilus treadmill we would consider second-hand, and only as a cheap walker.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="recall" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Nautilus T616 and T618 Recall: No Remedy Left</h2>
<p>
            We searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall database for Nautilus, Bowflex and
            Johnson Health Tech on 5 November 2026. One recall covers Nautilus treadmills, and it is serious for
            anyone buying used.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Recall 22-153, 9 June 2022:</strong> about 7,300 Nautilus T616 and T618 treadmills, which
            can start on their own, creating a fall hazard. Nautilus had 21 reports of self-starting and no
            injuries. They were sold <a href="/best-buy-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>at Best Buy</a>, Walmart, other stores, nautilus.com and Amazon from April through
            November 2021.</li>
            <li><strong>Affected serial numbers:</strong> T616 from 100672PRO21140001 to 100672PRO21171980; T618
            from 100647PRO21130111 to 100647PRO21183960. The model name is on the plastic shroud at the front of the
            belt and the serial number on the base frame beneath the belt.</li>
            <li><strong>Status now:</strong> the CPSC notice was updated to say that the firm is no longer in
            business and the remedy is no longer available. It tells owners to stop using and discard the product,
            and not to donate or resell it.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Some news coverage dates this recall to June 2023; the CPSC's own page and database give 9 June 2022.
            The other Nautilus-era recalls in the database cover Bowflex SelectTech barbells (2021) and BowFlex
            adjustable dumbbells (2025, issued by Johnson Health Tech) — no other treadmill.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What that means for a used T616 or T618</h3>
          <p>
            A treadmill inside those serial ranges should not be bought at any price, and a seller offering one is
            doing what the recall notice asks owners not to do. Units outside the ranges — earlier production, in
            particular — are not covered, but you still get no warranty and dwindling parts. If the seller cannot or
            will not show you the serial plate under the belt, walk away.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-used" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Buying a Used Nautilus Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            Most Nautilus treadmills on the market are five to ten years old, sold privately. That can still be a fair
            deal for a walker, provided you check the right things.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Read the serial number first.</strong> On a T616 or T618, compare it with the ranges above
            before anything else.</li>
            <li><strong>Assume no warranty.</strong> Nautilus warranties ran to the original buyer, and the company
            that gave them no longer exists. Johnson Health Tech did not take on the Nautilus brand.</li>
            <li><strong>Check parts.</strong> Search results for the parts supplier Sportsmith show a support page
            listing Nautilus treadmills as discontinued and no longer supported. Belts, motors and boards may still be
            found through third-party and appliance-parts sites, but a <a href="/treadmill-console-not-working/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>dead console</a> board can end the machine.</li>
            <li><strong>Run it.</strong> Walk and jog at your normal speeds, run the incline to the top and back,
            and listen for motor and roller noise. Fold it and unfold it; the hydraulic strut weakens with age.</li>
            <li><strong>Look under the belt.</strong> Wear on the deck and frayed belt edges are signs of high mileage
            or no lubrication.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill checklist</a> covers the inspection in detail, and <a href="/where-to-buy-a-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">where to buy a used treadmill</a> compares marketplaces, refurbishers and store returns. Before spending more than a few hundred dollars on
            an old Nautilus, read our <a href="/new-vs-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">new vs used treadmill guide</a>:
            a new Bowflex T6 on sale costs less than the T616 did, and comes with a warranty.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buy-instead" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What to Buy Instead of a Nautilus Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            The current Bowflex and Schwinn treadmills come from the same lineage as the Nautilus T-series: Nautilus
            Inc. built all three brands, and Johnson Health Tech now owns Bowflex and Schwinn. The cards below set out
            those machines with the prices on 5 November 2026. A used T614 cannot be bought on Amazon, so
            its card shows the Schwinn 810, the current machine with the T614's 20 by 55-inch deck. The Bowflex
            T6 and T9 are sold by Bowflex and its retailers rather than on Amazon, so their cards show the
            closest Amazon alternatives, both Horizons from the same parent group: the 7.0 AT, with the T6's deck
            and capacity, and the 7.4 AT, with the T9's.
          </p>
