        <div id="comparing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How the Replacements Compare with the Old Nautilus Line</h2>
<p>
            The T616 and T618 were good value in their day because they combined a 60-inch deck, a 15% incline and a
            long frame and motor warranty at about $1,150 and $1,500. On the day we checked, Bowflex's sale prices put
            the T6 at $999 and the T9 at $1,499, so a new machine with similar or better capacity now costs about what
            the Nautilus did.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Deck and capacity</h3>
          <p>
            Both Bowflex machines keep the 60-inch length runners need, and the T9 and T16 widen it to 22 inches. The
            T6's 325 lb rating beats the T616's 300 lb, and the T9 matches the T618's 350 lb. If capacity matters, our <a href="/best-treadmills-for-heavy-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for heavy people</a> guide explains why a stated limit is only part of the picture.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Motor ratings</h3>
          <p>
            Nautilus quoted continuous horsepower for the T-series. Bowflex's treadmill page quotes none, and our
            reviews note that Bowflex's figures elsewhere are test ratings. The numbers are not directly comparable;
            our <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill horsepower guide</a> explains continuous, peak and test ratings.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Warranty and subscriptions</h3>
          <p>
            Bowflex lists a lifetime motor warranty on its treadmill page; Schwinn lists 10 years on frame and motor
            and one year on everything else. Both push the JRNY app. The treadmills run without it, but some console
            features and classes need the subscription, so price that in. Our <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a> explains which terms matter most.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="amazon" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Are Nautilus Treadmills on Amazon?</h2>
<p>
            The T616 and T618 were sold on Amazon until 2021, and the recall notice lists Amazon among the sellers. We
            would not buy one there now: any listing still offering a new T616 or T618 would be old stock or
            third-party resale of a recalled product, and the T614 has long been unavailable. This page no longer cards
            a Nautilus model for that reason; the Schwinn 810 card takes the T614's place. Bowflex and Schwinn
            treadmills appear on Amazon at times, but the prices above are from Bowflex's and Schwinn's own stores,
            which also handle warranty registration directly. Our guide to <a href="/buying-a-treadmill-on-amazon/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">buying a treadmill on Amazon</a> explains how to check who the seller is.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying a Nautilus Treadmill</h2>
          <p>A used Nautilus can be cheap for good reason. These are the mistakes that turn a bargain into a problem.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Not checking a T616 or T618 serial number</h3>
              <p>About 7,300 units sold from April to November 2021 can start on their own, and the recall now has no remedy. The serial is on the base frame under the belt. If it falls in the ranges on this page, or the seller will not let you see it, do not buy the treadmill.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Expecting the warranty to transfer</h3>
              <p>Nautilus warranties were for the original buyer, and the company that issued them no longer exists. Johnson Health Tech bought the Bowflex business, not the Nautilus brand. Treat every used Nautilus as sold as-is.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Paying close to new-machine money</h3>
              <p>A new Bowflex T6 was $999 on sale on the day we checked, with a full warranty. A ten-year-old T616 with no support is worth a fraction of that. Price a used Nautilus as a walking machine you may have to scrap.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Confusing Nautilus home and commercial products</h3>
              <p>Nautilus strength machines in gyms are made under licence by Core Health & Fitness and have nothing to do with the home T-series treadmills. Support or parts for one will not help with the other.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">Nautilus no longer sells treadmills: the brand was sold in 2023, and the company
          behind the T614, T616 and T618 became BowFlex Inc. and was bought by Johnson Health Tech in 2024.</strong> Never buy a T616 or T618 from the April-November 2021 serial ranges; that recall has no remedy left. A used
          T614 is worth considering only as a cheap walker.
          </p>
          <p>
            For a new machine in the same spirit, the <a href="/bowflex-t6-treadmill/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">Bowflex T6</a> replaces the T616 at $999 on sale, and the <a href="/bowflex-t9-treadmill/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">Bowflex T9</a> matches the T618's capacity on a wider deck.
          </p>
        </div>

